From b10c8bb463392776f9840256b754b9e350c7abc9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josep Torra <n770galaxy@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:47:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 57/57] drm/gma500: fail an sgxfive render that overflowed its
 parameter buffer

A scene that needs more pages than its render context's parameter buffer
holds completed as if it had rendered: fence signalled, no fault, no hang,
but only part of the image drawn. With the PB shrunk through debugfs to
0x62, 0x40 or 0x23 pages, a 4096x4096 triangle drew 511520, 224448 and
43872 of its 4.4 million pixels. The firmware's out-of-memory path had
aborted a macrotile and run partial renders, which the driver does not
support: there is no colour reload in the background object and no depth
through ZLS, so the image is undefined.

The firmware counts both events in the RT data set: dword 3 each time the
TA runs out of pages, dword 4 each partial render it runs to free them.
On the hardware the partial-render count is non-zero exactly for the
scenes that overflowed (0x62 and 0x40 pages at 4096x4096; 0x23 pages at
64x64 and up) and zero for every size tried at the default 0x422 pages,
while the DPM abort status register is sticky across jobs and contexts.
The counters only grow, and a render context has one job in the firmware
at a time, so run_job clears both before the kick and the completion reads
the partial-render count before the fence signals: a job that needed a
partial render fails with -ENOSPC.

Nothing else changes: the firmware finished the job and its state is
consistent, so there is no reset, no error state, and the context is
neither lost nor charged to the file's fault budget; its next job is
judged alone. -ENOSPC tells this resource limit apart from the errors
of a recovery (-EIO, -ETIMEDOUT, -ECANCELED). Supporting partial renders
would replace it. The uapi header documents the error.

KUnit covers the counter offsets, the error, the per-job clear, and a
render context's fence failing with -ENOSPC while its next job and a
transfer context's job signal without an error.

Assisted-by: LLM
